Ready to defend, we laugh—
Firewalls watch, spirits rise;
Teamwork powers the fight.

There once were nurses so bright,
Who baked cupcakes each night;
Sweet treats will lift our fight.

Steady hands, steady minds;
Together we write hope into every strand.
Our science sings, fearless and kind.

Runways steady, crews united;
Together we keep the sky safe;
Our spirit lands on confidence.

Stars align, purpose steadies;
Our unity drives the brave flash;
Together we shield Earth’s horizon.

Lights dim, spirits rise;
Even silent tracks hear our beat;
Together we ride the city’s pulse.

Stars whisper courage;
Steady hearts chart silent arcs;
We stand firm, unshaken.

Wings steady, hearts aligned;
Every runway echoes calm resolve;
Together we guide the sky.

Steady pulse, calm breath;
In the roar we find our beat;
Hearts rise, walls fall.

Steady hearts, swift hands;
In the storm we stand as one;
Hope beats through the night.

Waves roar, hearts steady;
Steel and spirit hold the line;
We weather any storm.

Flames may roar, but we stay bold;
Together we stand, hearts of gold;
Fire can’t dim the spark we hold.

Runways hum, hearts steady;
Sky’s pulse meets steady breath;
We guide the wind together.

Steady minds amid the storm;
Our resolve keeps safety warm;
Together we stand, calm and sure;
Ready for any pressure we endure.

Draft a comprehensive 15‑year roadmap for strengthening the United States’ 9‑1‑1 emergency communications system. The plan should cover:
1. Upgrading legacy PSAP hardware and software to next‑generation IP‑based architectures; 
2. Implementing robust cyber‑hardening for call‑routing servers, GIS databases, and text‑to‑911 services; 
3. Building redundant, geographically‑diverse routing nodes and backup power supplies to ensure continuity during natural disasters or large‑scale outages; 
4. Deploying real‑time call‑authentication and fraud‑detection systems; 
5. Integrating next‑generation location‑accuracy technologies (e.g., NG911, indoor positioning, satellite‑based location services); 
6. Establishing a multi‑agency governance board, annual budget projections, key performance indicators, and risk‑assessment metrics for each phase (assessment, design, pilot, rollout, sustainment); 
7. Outlining phased implementation milestones, oversight audits, stakeholder‑engagement processes with state and local public‑safety agencies, and periodic review cycles.
Present the roadmap in a clear, organized format.

Prioritize grid stability: follow the incident runbook and halt any non‑essential changes;
Validate telemetry and access logs before isolating SCADA segments per approved playbooks;
Escalate anomalies immediately to incident command—no ad‑hoc actions outside documented procedures.

Life before metal: protect every person;
Clear, confirm, communicate—no assumptions;
Your calm, fast action breaks the chain of disaster.

Lives first, every switch and word;
Trust the checklist, speak up, stay heard;
Calm minds, clear steps—let safety lead every move.

Safety first: protect lives, preserve the aircraft;
Follow established emergency checklists, no deviations;
Communicate clearly, confirm instructions, and escalate any doubt immediately.

Prioritize airway, breathing, and massive hemorrhage first;
Divert limited surgical and ICU resources to salvageable criticals before expectant cases;
Reassess triage tags continuously and reallocate staff away from low‑acuity patients as surge intensifies.

Treat first those with breath and pulse yet bleeding fast;
Delay the weak and failing signs when numbers overwhelm;
Preserve what most can live if others must be passed.

Stop all non‑essential tasks, move to floors 42–48, and assist with evacuation only;
Keep smoke doors closed, guide people down the stairs, and do not use elevators under any circumstances;
If an area is filled with smoke or flames, do not enter—redirect occupants to a safer stairwell and report locations immediately.

Safety first, act fast and clear;
Follow procedure, stay right here;
Protect each life, report what you see;
No shortcuts, no panic—just discipline and duty.

Draft a comprehensive 15‑year roadmap for strengthening the university’s IT helpdesk communications and support‑ticket system. The plan should cover:
1. Upgrading legacy helpdesk hardware and software to next‑generation cloud‑based and IP‑based architectures;
2. Implementing robust cyber‑hardening for ticketing servers, authentication databases, and chat‑support services;
3. Building redundant, geographically‑diverse hosting options and backup power supplies to ensure continuity during building outages or regional network issues;
4. Deploying real‑time request‑authentication and spam‑detection systems;
5. Integrating next‑generation user‑support technologies (e.g., self‑service portals, knowledge‑base search, chatbots, and mobile app support); 
6. Establishing a multi‑department governance board, annual budget projections, key performance indicators, and risk‑assessment metrics for each phase (assessment, design, pilot, rollout, sustainment);
7. Outlining phased implementation milestones, oversight audits, stakeholder‑engagement processes with academic and administrative departments, and periodic review cycles.

Present the roadmap in a clear, organized format.
